            <ArticleTitle>[&quot;It was worth it when I saw his face&quot;: experiences of primiparous women during natural childbirth].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1590/1983-1447.2015.esp.56786</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1983-14472015000500143</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to know the experiences of primiparous women regarding the care practices provided by nursing professionals during natural childbirth.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">a qualitative, descriptive study, conducted with ten primiparous mothers at a maternity hospital in the interior of Rio Grande do Sul, between February and April 2014, based on individual semi-structured interviews. The data were submitted to thematic content analysis according to the operative proposal.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">the data were grouped into the following categories: The fear of not being able to give birth naturally and the encouragement of the nursing staff; The experience of pain in natural childbirth; Support versus distance; Good or bad childbirth experience? &quot;It was worth it in the end!&quot;</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">the provided care has a direct impact on the experience and how the women cope with the process of childbirth.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
